| FR-024 | An admin configures provider (Anthropic, OpenAI, Kimi, GLM), API key, model and reasoning level from a 
curated per-provider list whose parameters are valid for that provider, and a daily limit (1â€“200; default 20 for a 
company that never set it). Each save increments the settings revision. The key can be entered or replaced, never read 
back: every response, page, error, log, and Realtime payload exposes at most provider, model, reasoning level, limit, 
last four key characters, and who/when updated. | Must | AC-017 |
| FR-025 | Without a configured provider, manual notes work normally; AI controls are disabled with "An admin needs to 
configure AI"; admins also see a link to the settings. | Must | AC-018 |
| FR-026 | Search matches words in title, content, summary, and action-item descriptions, ignoring accents and case, 
in Portuguese and English text, only within the caller's company; filters by space and meeting-date period; orders by 
relevance then meeting date (newest first); paginates; shows matched excerpts. | Must | AC-014 |
| FR-027 | Export downloads a .md file with title, meeting date, space, author, participants, content converted to 
Markdown, summary, and action items (assignee, due date, status); the transcript is included only when "Include 
transcript" is ticked; user text is escaped so it cannot inject raw HTML. | Must | AC-019 |
| FR-028 | Deleting a space keeps its notes; they show "Space removed" and remain on the Meetings page, in search, and 
editable under the same rules. Deleting a company deletes its notes. | Must | AC-020 |
| FR-029 | Each note has an immutable company and an immutable author, set by the server from the creating user at 
creation and stored as historical fields (application `users.id` snapshot and display-name snapshot) separate from a 
nullable live reference to the user. Versions record their editor the same way. Historical fields are never changed by 
any client or process; deleting a user only clears live references, keeps notes, versions, and snapshots, never blocks 
the deletion, and never deletes company notes. | Must | AC-020, AC-023, AC-031 |
| FR-030 | Participants and assignees are stored by application `users.id` with a display-name snapshot. A person who 
is no longer a member of the note's company (company changed, removed, or user deleted) is shown as "Former member" 
(with the snapshot name), keeps their place in the note and its versions, and has no access. If the same `users.id` 
becomes a member of the same company again, their author/participant/assignee rights return. New selections accept 
current members only; saving or restoring may retain former members already present. | Must | AC-021 |
| FR-031 | Every processing start carries a server-issued, signed processing ticket (issued when the notice is shown, 
containing its issue time, valid for 24 hours, verifiable without stored records) and an idempotency key scoped to the 
authenticated application user, company, and operation, and bound to the note id, source note revision, transcript 
identity, and settings revision. A replay with the same key and binding returns the same pending or final result 
without a new dispatch (after re-authorizing the caller); a replay with a different binding is rejected; a ticket or 
key older than 24 hours is rejected as expired and never dispatched, whether or not any operation record still exists; 
replays within 24 hours (across midnight) are deduplicated by operation records (no transcript or draft content), kept 
for 30 days. An operation with no recorded outcome 10 minutes after dispatch (for example, after a server crash) 
becomes terminal "failed" and stays counted. Deleting a note or user never removes usage records or replenishes the 
limit. "Try again" is a new operation with a new key and a new admission. | Must | AC-028, AC-034 |

## 8. Business rules

| ID | Rule | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| BR-001 | Membership means the user's current `users.company_id` equals the note's company. All members can view all 
of their company's notes, versions, participants, action items, and transcripts; nobody else can. | Notes are company 
memory (r1 q4). |
| BR-002 | Any member can create a note in any space of their company, including private spaces. | r2 q11. |
| BR-003 | Edit rights: the author (while a member), current participants who are members, and company admins. Delete 
rights: the author (while a member) and company admins. Rights are evaluated on the state before the change, at the 
API and at the database. | r1 q5, r2 q11; prevents self-grant. |
| BR-004 | Editors may change the participant list, which changes who can edit; a viewer cannot add themselves; 
restore restores the version's participants under the same pre-change authorization. | Participants are versioned (r2 
q10). |
| BR-005 | Action items: editors create, edit, delete; editors and the current assignee toggle completion; nobody 
changes an item's note, creator, or creation time. | r2 q8. |
| BR-006 | AI suggestions never become content or assignments without explicit user Apply. | r1 q12. |
| BR-007 | Each accepted change creates one immutable version; restore creates a new version; action items and 
transcript are current-state only. | r1 q8, r2 q10. |
| BR-008 | The daily limit applies to every company, counts dispatched processings as in FR-022 (reprocessing 
included), defaults to 20, is adjustable by admins from 1 to 200, and resets at midnight America/Sao_Paulo. | r1 q11, 
r2 q4, r4. |
| BR-009 | Only admins configure AI. The API key is stored encrypted at rest in storage that no browser session can 
select, subscribe to, or decrypt; it is never stored in `companies.settings` or any client-readable table. | r2 q1, 
q3; review M6. |
| BR-010 | One transcript per note; a new upload replaces it immediately; the stored transcript is visible to every 
viewer of the note. | r1 q13, r2 q12, r4. |
| BR-011 | A change based on an outdated note revision is rejected. | r1 q18. |
| BR-012 | AI access uses API keys only; no subscription credentials are requested, stored, or proxied. | Anthropic 
policy; r3. |
| BR-013 | A processing uses the settings snapshot taken at admission; settings changes affect only later admissions; 
no silent fallback between providers. | Privacy notice fidelity; review H4. |

## 9. Data and integrations

### Data inputs

- Note fields, participants, rich-text content, action items, transcript files (.txt/.vtt/.srt, UTF-8 text, â‰¤1 MB).
- Admin AI settings: provider, API key, model, reasoning level, daily limit.

### Data outputs

- Stored notes, versions, participants, action items, transcripts, AI settings metadata, encrypted keys, usage records.
- AI drafts (transient until applied).
- Search results with excerpts; downloaded .md files.

### Integrations

- Anthropic Messages API (official SDK), OpenAI API (official SDK), Kimi (Moonshot) and GLM (Zhipu/Z.ai) through their 
OpenAI-compatible endpoints; each adapter sends only parameters valid for its provider and model (curated capability 
matrix). All calls are server-side from authenticated API routes.
- Supabase Postgres (RLS, full-text search), Supabase Auth.

### Database contracts (migrations required; local first)

- `meeting_notes`: add immutable `company_id`; author as historical id + name snapshots plus a live `created_by` 
reference (ON DELETE SET NULL); a revision column, content storage, and search vector; make `summary` optional; make 
`room_id` nullable with ON DELETE SET NULL; `company_id` â†’ companies ON DELETE CASCADE.
- New structures: note versions (historical fields immutable; editor historical id + name snapshot; live editor 
reference ON DELETE SET NULL is the only permitted change), note participants (with name snapshot), AI settings 
(metadata, revision), encrypted key storage (no client grants), daily usage reservations/records and operation records 
with idempotency keys (no content; 30-day retention).
- `meeting_note_action_items`: date-only due date (`date`), assignee name snapshot, per-item revision, completion-only 
path for assignees.
- RLS and grants for every table, with writes through authorized routes/RPCs; indexes for search (accent-insensitive 
Portuguese/English) and for list ordering.
- Legacy rows: before applying online, a read-only preflight on `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` counts existing 
`meeting_notes`/items; if any exist, the owner decides their company/author backfill before the migration is applied.
- Rollback: application first; database rollback must not restore the cascade that deletes notes with their space.
- The existing `IMeetingNoteRepository`, `SupabaseMeetingNoteRepository`, and `MeetingNote`/`ActionItem` types are 
reused and extended (no runtime caller exists today).

## 10. Constraints and assumptions

### Constraints

- Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase Postgres/Auth/RLS, TanStack Query, shadcn/ui, following AGENTS.md 
(server client in routes, `auth.getUser()`, `users.id` vs `users.supabase_uid`, roles `admin|member`).
- API keys and transcript text never appear in client code, logs, error messages, analytics, or reports; provider 
errors are mapped to sanitized messages.
- AI calls only from the server; the browser never calls a provider.
- Size limits: title â‰¤200 chars; content â‰¤100,000 chars; â‰¤50 participants; â‰¤100 action items per note; item 
description â‰¤1,000 chars; transcript â‰¤1 MB.
- UI in the app's current language (English); AI summary in the transcript's language.
- Migrations are written and applied locally during the phase; applied to the online database `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` 
only at phase end with the owner's explicit authorization, after the read-only preflight, followed by catalog readback 
and a runtime smoke check. Rollout: migrations, then app.
- Only integration and E2E tests (no unit tests); one test process at a time; one final Chrome-only E2E run.

### Assumptions

- The owner creates an OpenAI platform API key (billed separately from ChatGPT Plus/Pro) for the final test against 
the real provider (confirmed r3).
- Automated tests use a local fake AI server compatible with the provider APIs (confirmed r2 q17).

## 11. Edge cases and failure modes

| Case | Expected behavior |
|---|---|
| Two editors change the same note concurrently (edit, restore, attach, Apply) | The later change is rejected with a 
conflict warning showing the newer state (FR-007). |
| Editor edits an item while the assignee toggles completion | Each succeeds only against the item's current revision; 
the stale one gets a conflict. |
| Empty / >1 MB / wrong extension / binary file | Rejected before any change or AI call; not counted (FR-013). |
| .vtt/.srt with cue numbers and timestamps | Stripped; speaker names kept (FR-014). |
| Transcript too large for the configured model | Refused before dispatch with guidance; not counted (FR-020). |
| Provider error, timeout, refusal, malformed/truncated output | Note and transcript unchanged; error + "Try again"; 
counted if dispatched and processed (FR-021, FR-022). |
| Invalid key or provider unavailable before processing | Error + "Try again"; not counted (FR-022). |
| User leaves the page while processing | Draft discarded; counted if already dispatched (FR-022). |
| Several members start processing at 19/20 | At most one is admitted; the others are refused by the limit (FR-022). |
| Network retry of the start request | Same idempotency key and binding â†’ same result, no second dispatch or count; 
changed binding â†’ rejected (FR-031). |
| Server crash between dispatch and recording | Reservation stays counted (FR-022). |
| Provider accepts the request but the connection drops before the response | No automatic retry; counted; user sees 
error + "Try again" (new admission) (FR-016, FR-022). |
| Member is removed from the company while their processing runs | Draft discarded at return; Apply rechecks rights 
(FR-018, FR-021). |
| Another editor replaces the transcript or edits the note while a draft is pending | Apply refused as a conflict 
against the draft's source revision (FR-018). |
| Author or version editor's user row is deleted | Notes and history kept with id and name snapshots (FR-029). |
| Two items added at 99 items, or an add racing with Apply | At most one succeeds; total never exceeds 100; a refused 
Apply changes nothing (FR-011). |
| Member signs out or is removed while a response is in flight | Browser drops the late response; drafts and cached 
notes cleared (FR-021). |
| Browser replays a start request days later | Ticket expired â†’ rejected; never dispatched, even after record 
cleanup (FR-031). |
| Server crashes mid-processing | Operation becomes "failed" after 10 minutes, stays counted; Try again uses a new key 
(FR-031). |
| Admin changes provider/key/model after a member saw the notice | Start refused, new notice shown; already-admitted 
processings finish on their snapshot (FR-015, BR-013). |
| AI suggests a name matching no member or several members | Item unassigned (FR-017). |
| Suggestion duplicates an existing item | Unchecked, "already exists" (FR-018). |
| Apply would exceed 100 items | Refused with message (FR-018). |
| Daily limit reached | Refused with limit and reset time (FR-023). |
| No AI configured | Manual notes work; AI disabled with message; admin sees settings link (FR-025). |
| Space deleted | Notes kept, "Space removed" (FR-028). |
| Company deleted | Its notes are deleted (FR-028). |
| Participant/assignee leaves, is deleted, or rejoins | "Former member", no access; rights return on rejoin (FR-030). |
| Viewer tries to add themselves as participant (UI or direct DB) | Denied (BR-003, BR-004). |
| Assignee tries to change description, assignee, or note of an item | Denied (BR-005). |
| Transcript contains instructions aimed at the AI | Treated as data; output validated against a fixed schema; extra 
fields dropped; assignees resolved only against current members. |
| AI output contains HTML/script or Markdown injection | Sanitized in view, escaped in export. |
| Member of company A requests anything of company B (note, version, item, search, settings, usage) | Nothing returned 
/ denied (SM-3). |
| Non-admin calls the AI settings route | Denied (BR-009). |

## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations

- RLS on every new or changed table, comparing `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text` and scoping by the note's 
stored `company_id`; API routes resolve the application user by `supabase_uid`; authorization uses the pre-change 
state.
- Column-level protection: completion-only path for assignees; immutable `company_id`, `created_by`, `note_id`, 
creation times; versions are insert-only.
- API keys encrypted at rest in server-only storage (no authenticated/anon grants, no Realtime publication), decrypted 
only inside server code for a dispatch, never returned, never logged; provider error bodies sanitized.
- Rich text sanitized on write and render; AI output treated as untrusted; Markdown export escapes HTML.
- Only transcript text goes to a third-party provider (never the member roster), and only after the notice naming that 
provider for the current settings revision; the admin's provider choice (including providers hosted outside Brazil/US) 
is the company's decision.
- Abuse and cost: daily limit counted at dispatch with atomic reservations and idempotency; size and context 
preflight; server-side validation of every limit.
- Subscription credentials (Claude, ChatGPT) are never requested or stored.

## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations

- Editor, version list, review step, and dialogs are keyboard-operable with visible focus and labelled controls 
(Radix/shadcn primitives).
- Clear progress state while processing; errors state what happened and what to do.
- UI strings in English, matching the app; summary language follows the transcript; dates shown in the user's local 
time; the reset time shown in the user's local time.
- Interactive elements inside clickable cards follow the AGENTS.md interaction rules.

## 14. Acceptance criteria

- AC-001 (FR-001, FR-003, FR-005, BR-002): Given a member of company A, when they create a note in a private space 
with title, today's date, two participants, and content using bold, a heading, a list, a checklist, and a link, then 
it is listed first in that space's Notes tab and opens showing all fields with the formatting rendered; when they 
create a second note dated last month, then it is listed after the first.
- AC-002 (FR-001, FR-011, BR-001): Given an empty title, a missing date, a 201-char title, content above 100,000 
chars, 51 participants, or a participant who is not a current member of company A, when saving, then the save is 
rejected with a message and nothing is stored.
- AC-003 (FR-002, FR-027): Given content and AI output containing a script tag, an `onerror` attribute, and a 
`javascript:` link, when saved, viewed, and exported, then no script runs, the unsafe link is neutralized, and the 
export contains no raw HTML from user text.
- AC-004 (FR-006, BR-003, BR-004): Given a note by X with participant Y, when Y and an admin of company A edit it, 
then both succeed; when non-participant Z edits, adds themselves as participant, or restores a version â€” through the 
UI, the API route, and a direct Supabase client call â€” then each is denied and Z sees no edit control; when an admin 
of company B attempts the same, then it is denied.
- AC-005 (FR-007, BR-011): Given two sessions loaded the same revision, when each saves different content (and 
separately: one restores while the other saves; one attaches a transcript while the other applies a draft), then the 
first succeeds, the second gets a conflict warning showing the newer state, and the first's change is intact.
- AC-006 (FR-008, FR-009, BR-007): Given a note saved three times, when a viewer opens history, then three versions 
show who and when and cannot be modified by any role; when a non-editor viewer tries to restore, then it is denied; 
when an editor restores version 1, then a fourth version equals version 1's fields (including participants) and action 
items and the transcript are unchanged.
- AC-007 (FR-010, BR-003): Given a note with versions, items, participants, and a transcript, when the author confirms 
delete, then all of them are gone; when a participant who is neither author nor admin tries to delete, then it is 
denied.
- AC-008 (FR-011, BR-005): Given a note editor, when they add, edit, and delete an item with description, assignee, 
and due date, then each change is shown; when the assignee (not an editor) toggles completion, then it succeeds; when 
the assignee changes the description, assignee, due date, or note of the item, or toggles another person's item, then 
it is denied.
- AC-009 (FR-011): Given a note with 10 items, when adding an item with an empty or 1,001-char description, or with an 
assignee who left the company or belongs to another company, then each is rejected; given a note with 100 items, when 
adding a valid 101st, then it is rejected for capacity; a due date saved as 2026-11-05 is displayed as 2026-11-05 in 
every timezone.
- AC-010 (FR-012, FR-015, FR-016, FR-018, BR-006, BR-010): Given a configured provider (fake AI server in automated 
tests), when a member uploads a valid .txt via "New note from transcript", then the note exists immediately with title 
= file name, today's date, and the transcript; a notice names the provider; processing returns a draft that changes 
nothing until Apply; after editing the summary, reassigning one suggestion, rejecting one, and accepting another, 
Apply stores exactly the edited summary and the two accepted items and creates one version; when an editor attaches a 
new transcript to a note, then the stored transcript is replaced immediately and the summary is unchanged.
- AC-011 (FR-013): Given an empty file, a 1.1 MB file, a .pdf renamed .txt, a .docx, and a Latin-1 binary blob, when 
uploading, then each is rejected with a message, no note is created or changed, no AI call is made, and usage is 
unchanged.
- AC-012 (FR-014): Given a .vtt and a .srt with cue numbers, timestamps, cue settings, and speaker names, when 
processed, then the text received by the fake provider contains no cue numbers, timestamps, or cue settings and keeps 
speaker names and spoken text.
- AC-013 (FR-016, FR-017, BR-006): Given a draft suggesting "Ana" when exactly one current member is named Ana, 
"Bruno" when two members match, "Carla" when none does, and "Diego" who left the company, then only Ana is pre-linked 
and the others are unassigned; the request received by the fake provider contains the transcript and no member names 
that are absent from the transcript.
- AC-014 (FR-004, FR-026, BR-001, SM-2, SM-3): Given 1,000 notes in company A and notes in company B with the same 
words, when a member of A searches a word that appears only in a title, only in content, only in a summary, and only 
in an action-item description, in Portuguese with and without accents and in English with different case, with and 
without space and period filters, then each returns the matching notes of A within the filters, ordered by relevance 
then meeting date, paginated, with excerpts; the first page returns within 1 second locally; no note of B appears.
- AC-015 (FR-004): Given the dashboard, when the member clicks "AI Meeting Notes", then the Meetings page opens.
- AC-016 (FR-021, FR-022, FR-023, BR-008): Given the fake provider returns an error after processing, times out, 
refuses, or returns malformed JSON, then the note and transcript are unchanged, an error with "Try again" appears, and 
usage increases by one each; given it rejects the key or is unavailable before processing, then usage is unchanged; 
given the user navigates away after dispatch, then no draft is applied and usage increases by one; given a successful 
draft that the user never applies, then usage increases by one; given the company reached its limit, then processing 
is refused with the limit and reset time; at 00:00 America/Sao_Paulo the count resets.
- AC-017 (FR-024, BR-009): Given an admin, when they save provider, key, model, reasoning level, and limit 50, then 
the settings persist and the key is shown only as its last four characters; a canary key string never appears in any 
API response, company read, direct Supabase select (any role), Realtime payload, server log line, or client bundle; 
when a member calls the settings route, then it is denied; limit 0 or 201 is rejected; a company that never saved 
settings has limit 20.
- AC-018 (FR-025): Given no provider configured, when a member opens a note, then manual editing works and AI controls 
are disabled with "An admin needs to configure AI"; an admin additionally sees a link to settings.
- AC-019 (FR-005, FR-027): Given a note with formatted content, summary, items, and transcript, when exported without 
the option, then the .md contains title, date, space, author, participants, Markdown content, summary, and items but 
no transcript; with the option ticked, the transcript is included.
- AC-020 (FR-028, FR-029): Given a space with notes, when the space is deleted, then its notes remain on the Meetings 
page and in search labelled "Space removed", keep their author and company, and remain editable by the same people; 
when a company is deleted, then its notes are deleted.
- AC-021 (FR-030): Given a participant and assignee who leaves the company, then the note shows "Former member" with 
the snapshot name and that user can no longer read the note; when the user row is deleted, then the snapshot name 
still shows; when they rejoin the same company, then their participant rights return; saving the note while a former 
member is still listed succeeds.
- AC-022 (SM-1, BR-012): Given the owner's OpenAI API key configured on the new app against the online database, when 
processing the one-hour fixture transcript, then the draft appears within 60 seconds; at no point is a subscription 
login requested.
- AC-023 (SM-3, BR-001, BR-009, FR-029): Given two companies, when a member and an admin of each attempt direct API 
and database access to the other's notes, versions, participants, items, transcripts, search, AI settings, keys, and 
usage, then every attempt returns nothing or is denied, and `company_id`/`created_by` cannot be changed by any client.
- AC-024 (FR-012): Given an existing note, when a non-editor tries to attach a transcript, then it is denied and the 
stored transcript is unchanged.
- AC-025 (FR-015, BR-013): Given a member saw the notice for provider P at settings revision R, when the admin 
switches to provider Q before the member starts, then the start is refused and the notice for Q is shown; a processing 
already admitted under P completes on P.
- AC-026 (FR-016, FR-020, FR-024): Given each of the four provider adapters configured against the fake server, when 
processing, then each sends only its valid parameters for the selected model and reasoning level and returns a draft; 
given a transcript whose estimated size exceeds the configured model's budget, then it is refused before dispatch and 
usage is unchanged.
- AC-027 (FR-018, FR-019): Given a note with three items (one completed) and a stored transcript, when an editor 
reprocesses and the draft repeats one existing item and proposes two new ones, then the repeated one arrives unchecked 
as "already exists", Apply appends only the accepted new items, and the three existing items (including completion) 
are unchanged; when Apply would exceed 100 items, then it is refused.
- AC-028 (FR-022, FR-031): Given the company is at 19 of 20, when five processing requests start concurrently, then 
exactly one is dispatched and counted and four are refused; when a start request is replayed with the same key and 
binding (including after midnight America/Sao_Paulo), then the fake provider receives it once and usage counts it 
once; when the same key is sent for a different note or transcript, then it is rejected; when a member calls any usage 
table or RPC directly to refund or alter usage, then it is denied.
- AC-029 (FR-016, FR-022): Given the fake provider accepts a request and the connection drops before its response, 
then the fake provider records exactly one received request, usage increases by one, and the user sees an error with 
"Try again"; for each adapter, given the fake provider returns as its initial response one of that adapter's listed 
rejection status/type/code combinations with its normal error body, then usage is unchanged; given an unlisted status 
or code, or an overload error after streaming has begun, then usage stays counted.
- AC-030 (FR-018, FR-021): Given the fake provider is paused mid-processing, when another editor replaces the 
transcript, then Apply of the returned draft is refused as a conflict; when instead an admin removes the requesting 
member from the company, then no draft is returned to them and nothing is applied.
- AC-031 (FR-029): Given a note authored by X and edited by Y, when X's and Y's user rows are deleted, then the 
deletion succeeds, the note and all versions remain, author and editor show their snapshot names, and their historical 
id snapshots are unchanged; no client role can modify historical fields.
- AC-032 (FR-011, FR-018): Given a note with 99 items, when two editors add an item concurrently, then exactly one 
succeeds; given a note with 98 items where a manual add and a two-item Apply each succeed alone, when they run 
concurrently, then the total never exceeds 100 and a refused Apply changes nothing.
- AC-033 (FR-021, SM-3): Given a processing whose route response is delayed, when the member signs out and another 
account signs in (and separately when the member is removed from the company) before the response arrives, then the 
late response is dropped and no draft or note of the previous scope appears.
- AC-034 (FR-021, FR-031): Given an operation cancelled by leaving the page that later completes, when its key is 
replayed, then the status is "discarded" with no draft and no new dispatch; given a ticket issued more than 24 hours 
ago, then the request is rejected as expired without dispatch, including after its operation records have been 
deleted; given the server process is killed after dispatch, then 10 minutes later the operation is "failed", usage 
stays counted, and deleting the note does not change usage.

## 15. Test strategy

- Levels: integration and E2E only (AGENTS.md). No unit tests; no mocking of Supabase, repositories, or the code under 
test.
- Integration (Vitest against local Supabase and real API routes): RLS and route authorization per role and company, 
including direct-client attacks (AC-004, AC-006, AC-007, AC-008, AC-017, AC-021, AC-023, AC-024); validation limits 
(AC-002, AC-009, AC-011); revisions and conflicts with competing transactions (AC-005); versions (AC-006); transcript 
normalization (AC-012); assignee matching (AC-013); usage counting, reservations, idempotency, reset (AC-016, AC-028); 
settings revision binding (AC-025); outbound retries, refund classification, idempotency binding, replay, expiry and 
crash recovery, in-flight membership removal, draft source revision, user deletion, concurrent item capacity 
(AC-028â€“AC-032, AC-034); adapters and preflight (AC-026); reprocess merge (AC-027); search fields, languages, 
ordering, pagination, isolation, and timing with 1,000 seeded notes (AC-014); space and company deletion (AC-020); key 
canary (AC-017).
- External AI provider: replaced in automated tests only, by a local fake server speaking each provider's API, 
selected by environment configuration, recording received requests, with fixed responses and scripted failures (error 
after processing, timeout, refusal, malformed JSON, auth rejection, unavailable). Database, RLS, routes, and UI stay 
real.
- E2E (Playwright, two accounts): manual note lifecycle, rendering and sanitization, editing rights, conflict, 
history/restore, action items with assignee completion, transcript upload â†’ review â†’ Apply, reprocess, AI-disabled 
state, admin settings, search, export download, dashboard link, late responses across sign-out (AC-001, 
AC-003â€“AC-008, AC-010, AC-015, AC-018, AC-019, AC-027, AC-033).
- Test data: fictional transcripts in .txt/.vtt/.srt, Portuguese and English, including speaker names, accents, a 
one-hour fixture, and malformed files. No real personal data.
- Cadence: per task type-check + lint; per block type-check, lint, DB integration suite when server code changed; one 
final Chrome-only E2E run of all new specs plus the full regression suite, zero retries; one test process at a time.
- Owner test: AC-022 with a real OpenAI key against the online database after the migrations are applied.

