121: ## T9 — Usage accounting, admission, processing tickets, and idempotency contracts
122: Reasoning: Cost protection must hold under concurrency and replay before any provider call exists; this task owns 
what the database and ticket code can prove on their own (review P5). Dispatch-dependent evidence moves to T12.
123: Task: Server-only usage records per company per day (America/Sao_Paulo). One `service_role`-only admission RPC 
that, in a single transaction holding the company settings row lock: (1) verifies the caller's current membership and 
editor rights on the note (for replays too); (2) looks up an existing operation for the ticket's idempotency key — if 
found with the same binding, returns its status without new admission, regardless of later settings or source changes; 
if found with a different binding, rejects; (3) otherwise, with the note row locked, verifies that the bound settings 
revision is current and that the note's revision and transcript identity equal the ticket's (stale source → conflict, 
nothing reserved), reserves usage against the limit, records the operation with the provider/model/effort snapshot, 
and returns the matching key and the bound transcript text from the same snapshot. Settlement once, against the 
admission day, by a `service_role`-only RPC; it counts every dispatched operation, refunds only pre-processing 
rejections, and releases the reservation (not counted) for an operation that demonstrably never sent a request (size 
preflight refusal, cancellation before send, error before dispatch); settlement is idempotent. The idempotency key is 
generated by the server at ticket issuance and embedded in the signed ticket (a client cannot choose a key or reuse 
one with a new ticket); the key expires with its ticket, 24 h after issuance, checked before any lookup or admission. 
Idempotency keys scoped to user+company+operation and bound to note id, source revision, transcript identity, settings 
revision. Signed processing tickets (issue time, idempotency key, user, company, note, source revision, transcript 
identity, settings revision) with HMAC-SHA256 using the required server-only env var `MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET` 
(≥32 bytes, no fallback — AI start refuses with a configuration error when missing), plus optional 
`MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET_PREVIOUS` accepted for verification only so a rotation keeps outstanding tickets valid 
for their 24 h; the local value is provisioned in `.env.local` and `.env.example` documents it. A ticket-issue route 
(called when the notice is shown) authorizes current membership and editor rights before signing. Operation records 
without content kept 30 days (pg_cron cleanup); an operation without outcome 10 minutes after dispatch becomes 
"failed" and stays counted (pg_cron); deleting notes or users never changes usage; no client role can read-modify 
usage beyond reading its own company's count.
124: Done when: Admission, settlement, replay lookup, and ticket issuance satisfy FR-022/FR-023/FR-031 at the database 
and route boundary, independently of any provider.
125: Verify by: Integration tests: five concurrent admissions at 19/20 → exactly one reserved, four refused (AC-028); 
same key + binding returns the existing operation across midnight; different binding rejected; direct 
`anon`/`authenticated` calls to admission, settlement, and usage tables denied (AC-028); a settings save racing an 
admission yields either admission under the old revision with the old snapshot or refusal — never a mixed snapshot 
(AC-025 DB side); a transcript replaced after ticket issuance makes the new admission a conflict with nothing 
reserved; a released never-dispatched reservation leaves usage unchanged and a second settlement changes nothing; the 
stale-operation job turns an operation backdated >10 min into "failed", still counted; the cleanup job removes records 
older than 30 days; note deletion leaves usage unchanged (AC-034 DB side); limit-reached response data and reset at 
00:00 America/Sao_Paulo (AC-016 DB side); ticket-issue route denies a removed member and a non-editor and embeds a 
server-generated key; `supabase-rls-reviewer` audit.
