99: 
100: | ID | Requirement | Priority | Acceptance signal |
101: |---|---|---|---|
102: | FR-001 | A member creates a note in any space of their company, including private spaces, with title (required, 
1–200 chars), meeting date (required), participants (0–50, chosen from current company members), and rich-text content 
(≤100,000 chars). | Must | AC-001, AC-002 |
103: | FR-002 | The rich-text editor supports bold, italic, headings, bulleted and numbered lists, checklists, and 
links; content is sanitized on write and on render so no script, event handler, or unsafe URL runs. | Must | AC-003 |
104: | FR-003 | The space detail has a "Notes" tab listing that space's notes, ordered by meeting date, newest first. 
| Must | AC-001 |
105: | FR-004 | A "Meetings" page lists all notes of the company with search and filters; the dashboard "AI Meeting 
Notes" card links to it. | Must | AC-014, AC-015 |
106: | FR-005 | A note view shows title, meeting date, space (or "Space removed"), author, participants, rendered 
content, summary, action items, the stored transcript, and access to version history and export. | Must | AC-001, 
AC-019 |
107: | FR-006 | The author, current participants, and admins of the note's company can edit a note (fields, 
participants, transcript, AI draft application, restore); others cannot, at both the API and the database boundary. | 
Must | AC-004 |
108: | FR-007 | Each note carries a revision. Any change to note fields, participants, summary, or transcript, 
including restore and draft application, is accepted only against the current revision; a stale change is rejected 
with a conflict warning showing the newer state; nothing is overwritten silently. | Must | AC-005 |
109: | FR-008 | Every accepted change to title, meeting date, participants, content, or summary (including restore and 
draft application) creates one immutable version recording who and when. | Must | AC-006 |
110: | FR-009 | Any viewer lists versions and opens an old one; an editor restores it, which creates a new version 
with that version's title, date, participants, content, and summary. Restore does not change action items or the 
stored transcript. | Must | AC-006 |
111: | FR-010 | The author or an admin deletes a note after confirmation; deletion permanently removes the note, its 
versions, participants, action items, and transcript. | Must | AC-007 |
112: | FR-011 | Note editors create, edit, and delete action items (description required, 1–1,000 chars; one optional 
assignee chosen from current company members; optional date-only due date); editors and the current assignee toggle 
completion; an assignee who is not an editor can change nothing but completion; at most 100 items per note, enforced 
atomically for every insertion path (manual add and Apply) so concurrent writes can never exceed it. | Must | AC-008, 
AC-009, AC-032 |
113: | FR-012 | A member uploads a .txt, .vtt, or .srt transcript of at most 1 MB. "New note from transcript" creates 
the note at upload (title = file name without extension, truncated to 200 chars; date = today; editable) with the 
transcript stored. "Attach transcript" (editors only) replaces the stored transcript immediately. A note holds one 
transcript. | Must | AC-010, AC-024 |
114: | FR-013 | Empty, oversized, wrong-extension, or non-UTF-8/binary files are rejected before any note change or AI 
call with a clear message; nothing is stored and usage is unchanged. | Must | AC-011 |
115: | FR-014 | .vtt and .srt cue numbers, timestamps, and cue settings are removed before processing; speaker names 
and spoken text are kept. | Must | AC-012 |
116: | FR-015 | Before processing, the user sees a notice naming the provider that will receive the transcript text. 
The start request carries the AI-settings revision the notice was shown for; if the settings changed, processing is 
refused and the new notice is shown. | Must | AC-010, AC-025 |
117: | FR-016 | The server sends only the normalized transcript (no member roster) to the provider configuration 
snapshotted at admission (provider, key, model, reasoning level), with no fallback to another provider and no 
automatic SDK/transport retries, and returns a draft containing a summary (key points, decisions, next steps) in the 
transcript's language and up to 100 suggested action items with assignee names as written in the transcript. | Must | 
AC-010, AC-026, AC-029 |
118: | FR-017 | Suggested assignee names are resolved locally against current company members: an item is pre-linked 
only when the name matches exactly one current member by full display name or, failing that, by a first name unique 
among current members (case- and accent-insensitive); otherwise it is unassigned. | Must | AC-013 |
119: | FR-018 | The draft is transient and carries its source: the note revision and stored-transcript identity at 
generation start. The user edits the summary and accepts, edits, reassigns, or rejects each suggested item; "Apply" is 
accepted only if the note is still at that source revision (otherwise conflict, FR-007), rechecks the caller's rights 
inside the transaction, and atomically writes the summary, appends the accepted items, and creates one version, or 
changes nothing. Suggestions whose description matches an existing item of the note (case- and accent-insensitive) 
arrive unchecked and marked "already exists"; existing items are never modified or removed by a draft; Apply is 
refused if the resulting total exceeds 100 items. | Must | AC-010, AC-027, AC-030 |
120: | FR-019 | An editor can reprocess the stored transcript; the result is a new draft under FR-018; the summary and 
items change only on Apply. | Must | AC-027 |
121: | FR-020 | Before dispatch, the server estimates the input size against the configured model's context and output 
budget; a transcript that does not fit is refused with a message naming the limit and suggesting a smaller file or a 
larger-context model; not counted. | Must | AC-026 |
122: | FR-021 | If processing fails (provider error, timeout, refusal, malformed or truncated output, invalid key), 
the note and transcript remain as they were, the user sees what happened with "Try again", and no draft is applied. 
Leaving the page cancels the browser request; the server makes a best-effort abort of the outbound provider request 
and discards the draft; counting follows FR-022 regardless of whether the abort succeeds. Before returning a draft, 
the server rechecks that the caller is still a member of the note's company and the note still exists; otherwise the 
draft is discarded. A discarded or cancelled operation keeps no draft content; its replay returns the terminal status 
"discarded" without dispatch. In the browser, drafts and note data are scoped to the signed-in application user and 
company; on sign-out, removal from the company, or company change, pending requests are cancelled, drafts and cached 
notes are cleared, and late responses for the previous scope are dropped. | Must | AC-016, AC-030, AC-033 |
123: | FR-022 | Usage: each provider dispatch counts toward the company's daily limit, whether it succeeds, times out, 
returns unusable output, or the user leaves. A dispatch is refunded only when the provider's initial HTTP response is 
a non-2xx rejection (with its normal error body) whose status/type/code is listed for that adapter in the capability 
matrix as authentication/permission, rate-limit, overload, or unavailable; errors inside an accepted (2xx or 
streaming) response, unknown statuses or codes, and connection loss after the request was sent stay counted. Not 
counted at all: files rejected by FR-013, inputs refused by FR-020, failures before dispatch. Admission atomically 
reserves one unit against the limit before dispatch; concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit; each reservation 
covers exactly one dispatch and settles at most once against the day of its admission; a reservation without a 
recorded outcome (server crash) stays counted; only server code settles reservations — no client role can classify, 
refund, or alter usage. | Must | AC-016, AC-028, AC-029 |
124: | FR-023 | When the company's count for the current day (midnight to midnight, America/Sao_Paulo) has reached its 
limit, processing is refused with a message stating the limit and the reset time. | Must | AC-016 |
125: | FR-024 | An admin configures provider (Anthropic, OpenAI, Kimi, GLM), API key, model and reasoning level from a 
curated per-provider list whose parameters are valid for that provider, and a daily limit (1–200; default 20 for a 
company that never set it). Each save increments the settings revision. The key can be entered or replaced, never read 
back: every response, page, error, log, and Realtime payload exposes at most provider, model, reasoning level, limit, 
last four key characters, and who/when updated. | Must | AC-017 |
126: | FR-025 | Without a configured provider, manual notes work normally; AI controls are disabled with "An admin 
needs to configure AI"; admins also see a link to the settings. | Must | AC-018 |
127: | FR-026 | Search matches words in title, content, summary, and action-item descriptions, ignoring accents and 
case, in Portuguese and English text, only within the caller's company; filters by space and meeting-date period; 
orders by relevance then meeting date (newest first); paginates; shows matched excerpts. | Must | AC-014 |
128: | FR-027 | Export downloads a .md file with title, meeting date, space, author, participants, content converted 
to Markdown, summary, and action items (assignee, due date, status); the transcript is included only when "Include 
transcript" is ticked; user text is escaped so it cannot inject raw HTML. | Must | AC-019 |
129: | FR-028 | Deleting a space keeps its notes; they show "Space removed" and remain on the Meetings page, in 
search, and editable under the same rules. Deleting a company deletes its notes. | Must | AC-020 |
130: | FR-029 | Each note has an immutable company and an immutable author, set by the server from the creating user 
at creation and stored as historical fields (application `users.id` snapshot and display-name snapshot) separate from 
a nullable live reference to the user. Versions record their editor the same way. Historical fields are never changed 
by any client or process; deleting a user only clears live references, keeps notes, versions, and snapshots, never 
blocks the deletion, and never deletes company notes. | Must | AC-020, AC-023, AC-031 |
131: | FR-030 | Participants and assignees are stored by application `users.id` with a display-name snapshot. A person 
who is no longer a member of the note's company (company changed, removed, or user deleted) is shown as "Former 
member" (with the snapshot name), keeps their place in the note and its versions, and has no access. If the same 
`users.id` becomes a member of the same company again, their author/participant/assignee rights return. New selections 
accept current members only; saving or restoring may retain former members already present. | Must | AC-021 |
132: | FR-031 | Every processing start carries a server-issued, signed processing ticket (issued when the notice is 
shown, containing its issue time, valid for 24 hours, verifiable without stored records) and an idempotency key scoped 
to the authenticated application user, company, and operation, and bound to the note id, source note revision, 
transcript identity, and settings revision. A replay with the same key and binding returns the same pending or final 
result without a new dispatch (after re-authorizing the caller); a replay with a different binding is rejected; a 
ticket or key older than 24 hours is rejected as expired and never dispatched, whether or not any operation record 
still exists; replays within 24 hours (across midnight) are deduplicated by operation records (no transcript or draft 
content), kept for 30 days. An operation with no recorded outcome 10 minutes after dispatch (for example, after a 
server crash) becomes terminal "failed" and stays counted. Deleting a note or user never removes usage records or 
replenishes the limit. "Try again" is a new operation with a new key and a new admission. | Must | AC-028, AC-034 |
